        "branch_id": "b3",
        "command": "touch mirror",
        "response": "As you touch the mirror, it slowly begins to pull you through. You try to \nresist, but unfortunately it's grip is too powerful. After a few seconds, the \nmirror sucks you through completely and you find yourself sitting down in a \ngrassy plain. As you look up, you have to shield your eyes from the intense \nglow of the sun.\n\nThe sun? You must be back on the surface! You look around and quickly recognize \nyour surroundings as your farm in Amylya! You run toward your farmhouse and are \ngreeted by your shocked parents. Your mother gives you a big hug and quietly \nweeps, \"Thank the gods, thank the gods.\" Your father rushes over to pat you on \nthe back. You follow your parents inside, relate a few stories, and sleep in \nyour bed for the first time in over a month. You realise now how much you had \nmissed this in the last month, and how you now hope you never have to leave \nagain.\n\nWhen you awake, you go to the Amylyan Druids' Temple, where you are met with a \nhero's welcome. Word had gotten out of your return, and citizens and clergy \nalike are waiting at the Temple for word of your adventure and the Jewel. \nAllarah, the Amylyan High Priest, approaches you and congratulates you on your \nexpedition.\n\n\"After your party entered the Earth's crust, another ten parties soon followed. \nYou are the only member of any party to have returned.\"\n\n\"The only one?\" you say with alarm.\n\n\"Yes, unfortunately. Now please tell us of your travels and the Jewel!\" \n\nAs you prepare to give your speech, you look around the crowd at all the \ndifferent people that are in attendance. Some are old, some are children; some \nare wealthy estate owners, others poor beggars. Your sights, however, become \ntransfixed on one group: a group of five adventurers who can't be a day older \nthan twenty-one. A banner that one of them carries reads:\n\n\"Jewel Expedition No. 94\"\n\nThese young adventurers must be the next group that will enter the underground \nin search of the Jewel. Your stomach sinks a little bit, and you begin your \nspeech.\n\nYou tell the audience about your travels. You tell them about a disruptive \ngeyser, a flooding river, and a magical coat that saved your skin on more than \none occasion. You tell them about three fearsome dragons and how much you've \nended up regretting ever trespassing in their domains. And last, you tell them \nabout the Jewel. It's just a myth, you say. There is no Jewel, and if there was \none, it was either stolen or is in some other part of the underground. When the \ncollective group hears this, there is a resounding sense of shock and dismay.\n\n\"How could this be?\" starts Allarah. \"Our druids were sure that the Jewel was \nbeing protected by the Red Dragon!\"\n\n\"The Red Dragon is a mistress of trickery, High Priestess. I think she may have \nsent you false information on purpose. The truth is, I found no Jewel.\"\n\nAllarah looks at you incredulously and the throng of people begins to disperse. \nThis effectively ends your hero's welcome. You step down from the podium and \nhead home.\n\n*********\n\nYears later, you find yourself with your own family. You have two children and \na loving spouse. You've also inherited most of the land that your father once \ntilled. You still impress young children with fantastic stories about the \nunderground, but you are by no means regarded as any sort of hero. The \nexpeditions into the Earth's crust have more or less stopped in recent years. \nEvery couple of years a few parties of adventurers attempt to enter the \nunderground in search of the Jewel, but none ever return. This of course only \nincreases public wariness about entering the underground.\n\nOne day, just after the harvest season, you make your way back to the sinkhole \nthat first led you beneath the surface. It is now covered with signs warning \npotential spelunkers about the dangers of cave travelling and the perils that \nawait. You smile briefly as you remember your friendship with Jacob and Ariana \nand all the good times you had. You peer into the sinkhole and see nothing but \nendless dark. Nothing ever comes back from that hole.\n\nNothing, but the lingering scent of foolish dreams.\n\n\n    *** You have won ***\n\n\nIn that game you scored 90 out of a possible 90, in 243 turns, earning you the \nrank of Humbled Land Owner.\n\nWould you like to RESTART, RESTORE a saved game, give the FULL score for that \ngame or QUIT?\n Jewel Room                                          Score: 90    Moves: 243\n\n\n\n                  My name is Ozymandias, king of kings.\n                  Look on my works ye mighty, and despair.\n                  Nothing beside remains.  Round the decay\n                  Of the colossal wreck, boundless and bare\n                  The lone and level sands stretch far away.\n\n                       -Shelley\n[Score 90, up 10]\n> ",
